---
/**
 * Starlight's sidebar, with the platform chips above it.
 *
 * AN OVERRIDE THAT DELEGATES. The tree itself, its persistence across navigations and its mobile
 * behaviour are Starlight's; re-implementing any of that to gain a row above it would mean owning
 * three things to add one.
 *
 * The filter sits ABOVE the tree and inside the same scroll container, so it scrolls away with the
 * documentation rather than pinning a control the reader has already used. The notice it raises
 * when something is hidden is the part that must not scroll out of reach, and that one is repeated
 * on the page itself.
 *
 * IT USED TO BE ABSENT IN THE API/MCP MANUAL, AND OLEH OVERRULED THAT ON 2026-08-25: "the client
 * asked for it, because the content there can differ per platform." The old argument is kept below
 * because it is the one to re-read if this ever looks wrong, and because the objection it raised is
 * real and still unpaid:
 *
 *   "`routeData.ts` gives that manual its own two-group tree, and not one page in it carries a
 *    `platform` frontmatter field — so the control would render, accept a choice, and change nothing
 *    visible. A filter that demonstrably does nothing teaches a reader that the filter does nothing,
 *    which is a lesson they then carry back into the product manual where it is false."
 *
 * WHAT ACTUALLY ANSWERS IT is that the filter is a SCOPE, not a promise that this page varies. Of the
 * ninety pages in the product manual, fifteen name no platform at all and the control has always
 * coexisted with them: a reader standing on one of those sets a scope and sees nothing move, and that
 * has never taught anybody the control is broken. The API manual is the same situation with a
 * different count, and the count changes the day an authorization page is written per platform, which
 * is the change the client is asking us to be ready for. Nothing here has to change then.
 *
 * SO THE HONEST STATEMENT OF WHAT SHIPS TODAY: the control appears above the product and API/MCP
 * trees and filters nothing in the API one, because no page there declares a `platform` yet. Verify
 * that with a count rather than by eye — `grep -rl '^platform:' src/content/docs/api
 * src/content/docs/mcp` is zero today and is the thing to re-run before assuming this is broken.
 *
 * ── IT IS STILL ABSENT IN THE CHANGELOG, AND FOR A REASON THE ARGUMENT ABOVE DOES NOT COVER ────
 * The scope argument works because a documentation page CAN be about one platform, so a reader
 * narrowing the manual is asking a question the manual can answer. A changelog entry cannot be: it
 * is a dated record of something that happened, and a release either happened or it did not. There
 * is no version of it that is true for Airtable and false for Notion, so there is nothing for a
 * scope to select. That is a different claim from "no page carries the field yet" — this one does
 * not become false when somebody writes more pages.
 *
 * SUPPRESSED BY MANUAL, NOT BY PREFIX. `manualForPath` is the registry three other files already
 * agree through (`lib/api-docs.ts` carries the argument); testing `!== 'changelog'` here rather than
 * matching `/changelog` means a fourth manual has to opt IN, which is the safer direction to be
 * wrong in for a control whose failure mode is silently filtering nothing.
 */
import Default from '@astrojs/starlight/components/Sidebar.astro';
import PlatformFilter from './PlatformFilter.astro';
import { manualForPath } from '../lib/api-docs';

const showFilter = manualForPath(Astro.url.pathname) !== 'changelog';
---

{showFilter && <PlatformFilter />}
<Default><slot /></Default>
